{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 10, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a powerful ruler of the County of Tripoli during the late 12th and early 13th centuries. He is a significant figure in the history of the Crusades and the Levant. Born around 1130 Raymond III succeeded his father Raymond II as Count of Tripoli in 1152. His reign was marked by both military expansion and internal political maneuvering.\n\n*Early Reign and Military Campaigns*\n\nRaymond III focused on consolidating his control over the County and expanding its territory. He engaged in several conflicts with the Seljuk Turks and other neighboring powers. A major part of his early reign involved defending Tripoli against Seljuk incursions. He successfully repelled several attacks maintaining the city's strategic importance. Raymond III also participated in the Third Crusade though his involvement was relatively limited. He served as a knight under King Louis VII and participated in the Siege of Acre but did not play a decisive role in the overall campaign. His military successes solidified Tripoli's position as a key power in the Levant.\n\n*Political and Economic Developments*\n\nRaymond III's reign also saw significant political and economic advancements. He strengthened the County's administration and fostered trade. Tripoli became a major center for commerce connecting Europe with the East. He invested in infrastructure including building fortifications and improving roads. His policies promoted economic growth benefiting the local population. Raymond III also dealt with internal power struggles and maintained a delicate balance of power within the County. He navigated relationships with various factions ensuring stability and preventing internal conflicts.\n\n*Legacy and Later Life*\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 at the age of 57. His death marked a turning point in the history of Tripoli. His legacy is one of military stren